        <body>
            <div xml:id="d23">

                <pb facs="0120" n="874" sameAs="frus:frus1917Supp02v02/pg874"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 600.119/427</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of the Law Adviser for the Department of
                        State</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Woolsey</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1917-05-25">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 25, 1917</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Mr. Woolsey read the
                    following to Lord Percy,
                    but did not give him a copy: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>As American export control legislation is still pending in Congress and
                            may be modified prior to enactment into law, all that can be said at the
                            present time in regard to “bunker control” is that if the United States
                            is given legislative authority, it is the present tentative view of the
                            Secretary of State that the most feasible method of exercising <pb
                                facs="0121" n="875" xml:id="pg875"/> bunker control is that set
                            forth in the first British proposal, paragraph (<hi rendition="#italic"
                                >a</hi>) of the attached draft memorandum dated May 24, 1917, with
                            the addition at the end of the following proviso: <quote
                                rend="blockquote">
                                <p>
                                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Provided</hi>, That either
                                    Government has liberty to propose additions to or removals from
                                    the “coal white list,” and in the event of disagreement,
                                    reserves the right to export coal to such additional persons
                                    (not on the “coal white list”) who subscribe to the “Conditions
                                    of Supply of Cargo Coal” agreed upon by the two Governments, or
                                    to refuse to export coal to any persons regarded as
                                    undesirable,</p>
                            </quote>
                        </p>
                        <p rendition="#flush">with the reservations made by the United States
                            Government in paragraphs (<hi rendition="#italic">c</hi>), (<hi
                                rendition="#italic">e</hi>), and (<hi rendition="#italic">f</hi>) of
                            said attached draft memorandum.</p>
                        <p>The United States, however, does not commit itself to accepting this
                            proposal, or limit its freedom of action in accepting the second
                            proposal or modifications of these proposals.</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>


                <xi:include href="d23-a1.xml" xpointer="d23-a1"/>
